{"name":"Panama hat","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Panama_hat.jpg/960px-Panama_hat.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panama_hat.jpg","caption":"Panama hat","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A Panama hat is a woven straw hat made from the toquilla plant, traditionally in Ecuador despite the name. Its light colour and breathable weave make it a staple of tropical and summer menswear, often paired with linen or silk suits.","For appearance, it frames the face, adds a polished warm-weather accessory and gives shade. Quality is judged by weave fineness, and the finest hats can take months to make."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Wearers choose a fedora-style shape and a weave grade, then wear it with summer tailoring or casual beachwear. Finer weaves are lighter and more flexible; cheaper hats use coarser weave or other materials."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No health risks are documented for wearing one. Fine hand-woven hats can cost hundreds to thousands of dollars, and lower-cost machine or foreign-made versions are not equivalent in quality, according to descriptions of the trade."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Panama_hat.jpg/960px-Panama_hat.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Panama_hat.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Panama hat","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","creator":"Hex","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panama_hat.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Panama_hat_%28drawing%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail