e":"T Nation · 185 posts · 2007"},{"title":"Centipede Eats Mouse!","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/centipede-eats-mouse/67127","site":"T Nation · 165 posts · 2006"},{"title":"Conservatives Hate Thinking","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/conservatives-hate-thinking/181413","site":"T Nation · 161 posts · 2012"},{"title":"The World Wants Obama","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/the-world-wants-obama/113887","site":"T Nation · 152 posts · 2008"}],"news":[],"social":[],"web":[{"title":"Panama hat — Grokipedia","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Panama_hat"},{"title":"Panama hat","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panama_hat"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Jawline & face shape","chain":["Wear a fine-woven straw hat","Face is shaded and framed","A polished, classic summer look"],"effect":"direct","text":"The hat completes a warm-weather outfit and shades the face. Wearers pick brim and shape to suit their face and style."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Panama_hat","lead":["The Panama hat comes from Ecuador despite its name. It is traditionally made from the plaited leaves of the toquilla or jipijapa plant, Carludovica palmata, and the term appears in print from 1828 [1].","Demand grew during the California Gold Rush, when travellers crossing the Isthmus of Panama needed sun protection, and Theodore Roosevelt's 1906 photograph in one at the Panama Canal boosted its popularity [1]. In the 1920s and 1930s lighter fedora-like Panamas displaced the straw boater as the favoured summer hat [1].","Today the hat is a style accessory: light-coloured and breathable, often worn with linen or silk summer suits [1]. UNESCO added the traditional weaving art to its Intangible Cultural Heritage list in December 2012 [1]."],"infobox":[["Type","Woven straw hat"],["Material","Toquilla straw (Carludovica palmata)"],["Origin","Ecuador"],["Price","About $450 to $10,000 abroad for fine hats"],["Grades","Measured in weave tightness; Montecristi is the finest"]],"sections":[{"h":"Design and grades","paras":["Quality depends on how tight the weave is, measured with a frame tool and counted in carreras. The best hats, called Montecristis, can have up to 3,000 weaves per square inch [1]. A master weaver may spend up to eight months on one hat [1]."],"subs":[]},{"h":"History","paras":["Spanish conquistadors saw coastal Ecuadorians in toquilla-straw hats in 1526, and hat weaving was a cottage industry by the mid-1600s [1]. About 220,000 hats a year were imported to the US by 1850, and they were shown at the 1855 Paris World's Fair [1]."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"1526","event":"Spanish conquistadors see coastal Ecuadorians wearing toquilla-straw hats [1]."},{"year":"1828","event":"The term Panama hat appears in print [1]."},{"year":"1906","event":"Theodore Roosevelt is photographed in one at the Panama Canal [1]."},{"year":"2012","event":"Traditional weaving is added to UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list [1]."}],"refs":[{"title":"Panama hat","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panama_hat","source":"Wikipedia","year":2024,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Panama hat","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Panama_hat","source":"Grokipedia","year":2025,"type":"encyclopedia"}],"studies":[],"reddit":[],"forums":[{"title":"Trump 2025 - Resuming The National Nightmare Of Peace And Prosperity (Part 1)","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/trump-2025-resuming-the-national-nightmare-of-peace-and-prosperity-part-1/289108","site":"T Nation · 9998 posts · 2024"},{"title":"NFL 2013","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/nfl-2013/192058","site":"T Nation · 1124 posts · 2013"},{"title":"Things You Hate That Everyone Loves","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/things-you-hate-that-everyone-loves/167289","site":"T Nation · 1070 posts · 2011"},{"title":"Ron Paul Revolution","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/ron-paul-revolution/170012","site":"T Nation · 1056 posts · 2011"},{"title":"If America Should Go Communi